  "argument_markdown": "## Scope and exact theorem\n\nSubproblem C66-GEO-Z only: construct and identify the evaluation-failure locus Z in the balanced tetragonal Casnati\u2013Ekedahl geometry of packet C66-001-v3. The campaign exact target W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q)\u2245W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21) being \u2295Q(8) is not claimed here. No dependency artifacts are supplied (TASK.json: blocked_dependencies=[], dependency_artifacts={}), so nothing is imported from later DAG nodes C66-GEO-H0, C66-GEO-COMP, or Tate/counterexample nodes.\n\nBlocked routes vcd-only-vanishing, undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex, point-count-without-degree-parity-separation, and forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown are not used. Working-context candidates are treated as unproved and are not cited as evidence.\n\n## Source-backed inputs\n\n1. Packet CE convention and balanced splitting (C66-001-v3 canonical geometric bottleneck): F=O_{P^1}(4)\u2295O_{P^1}(5); W_5:=(O_{P(E^\u2228)}(2)\u2297\u03b3^*O_{P^1}(-5))|_C is the f_2=5 summand of (O(2)\u2297\u03b3^*F^\u2228)|_C, not the O_{P^1}(5) summand of F; six-point failure condition stated as O_C(\u0393)\u2245W_5\u2297\u03c9_C^{-1}; ordered distinct reduced divisors modeled as a finite \u00e9tale S_6-cover of the reduced-support open (explicitly not closed).\n\n2. SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 (packet primary locator): for W_f=(O_{P(E^\u2228)}(2)\u2297\u03b3^*O(-f))|_C one has deg(W_f|_C)=4g+12\u22124f, the obstruction splits as H^0(\u03c9_C\u2297W^\u2228(\u0393))=\u2295_i H^0(\u03c9_C\u2297W_{f_i}^\u2228(\u0393)), and deg(\u03c9_C\u2297W_f^\u2228(\u0393))=4f+n\u22122g\u221214.\n\n3. FND-0001 (mechanically_verified): for d=3g\u22123+n, W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{g,n})\u2245W_{2d\u221216}H^{2d\u221216}(M_{g,n})(d). Used only as ambient dictionary; not needed for the geometric identification of Z.\n\n4. FND-0005 (corroborated for (6,6)): proposed evaluation-failure locus nonempty by the indicated Riemann\u2013Roch computation, subject to the independent confirmation recorded in (E) below.\n\n## Proved steps\n\n### Step 1: Degree arithmetic for g=6\n\nSubstitute g=6 into SRC-0004: deg(W_5)=4\u00b76+12\u22124\u00b75=16 and deg(W_4)=4\u00b76+12\u22124\u00b74=20. With deg(\u03c9_C)=2g\u22122=10 one obtains deg(L)=deg(W_5\u2297\u03c9_C^{-1})=16\u221210=6.\n\nFor n=6 and an effective degree-six divisor \u0393, SRC-0004 gives\n\ndeg(\u03c9_C\u2297W_5^\u2228(\u0393))=4\u00b75+6\u22122\u00b76\u221214=0,\n\ndeg(\u03c9_C\u2297W_4^\u2228(\u0393))=4\u00b74+6\u22122\u00b76\u221214=\u22124.\n\n### Step 2: Vanishing of the f=4 obstruction (claim A)\n\nOn a smooth projective connected curve, a line bundle of negative degree has H^0=0. Hence H^0(C,\u03c9_C\u2297W_4^\u2228(\u0393))=0 for every effective degree-six \u0393 on every smooth geometric fibre. The CE obstruction therefore reduces entirely to the f=5 summand.\n\n### Step 3: f=5 obstruction criterion (claim B)\n\nThe line bundle \u03c9_C\u2297W_5^\u2228(\u0393) has degree zero. On a smooth projective connected curve, a degree-zero line bundle has a nonzero global section if and only if it is isomorphic to O_C. Equivalently,\n\nH^0(C,\u03c9_C\u2297W_5^\u2228(\u0393))\u22600  \u21d4  \u03c9_C\u2297W_5^\u2228(\u0393)\u2245O_C  \u21d4  O_C(\u0393)\u2245W_5\u2297\u03c9_C^{-1}=L.\n\nThis is exactly the packet six-point failure condition, derived from the f_2=5 summand of SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 including the sign in \u03b3^*O(\u22125).\n\n### Step 4: Set-theoretic identification Z^{geo}=I^{geo} (claim C)\n\nBy Steps 2\u20133 and the splitting of the CE obstruction in SRC-0004,\n\nH^0(C,\u03c9_C\u2297W^\u2228(\u0393))\u22600  \u21d4  H^0(C,\u03c9_C\u2297W_5^\u2228(\u0393))\u22600  \u21d4  O_C(\u0393)\u2245L.\n\nTherefore the geometric evaluation-failure locus Z^{geo} coincides set-theoretically with the Abel\u2013Jacobi incidence locus I^{geo} of geometric points of Conf_6 (or of the relative effective-divisor space) satisfying O_C(\u0393)\u2245L. This is the identification required by C66-GEO-Z at the geometric-point level.\n\n### Step 5: Reduced-support open and finite \u00e9tale S_6-cover (claim D)\n\nIn the relative effective-divisor space of degree six over B^{bal}, the condition that the support be six distinct reduced points is the complement of the relative discriminant (non-reduced or coincident support). That complement is open; it is not closed. Over that open, the map from the stack of six ordered pairwise-distinct sections to the unordered reduced divisor is a principal S_6-bundle (finite \u00e9tale S_6-torsor): the S_6-action freely permutes ordered distinct labels. Consequently the ordered reduced failure locus is the preimage of the reduced-support open of the residual incidence for L, matching the packet model. (On coarse moduli spaces with residual automorphisms the freeness may fail; the torsor statement is on the stack of pairwise-distinct sections, as required by the packet bottleneck.)\n\n### Step 6: Fibrewise nonemptiness of |L| (claim E)\n\nRiemann\u2013Roch on a smooth genus-6 fibre: \u03c7(L)=deg(L)\u2212g+1=6\u22126+1=1, so h^0(L)=1+h^1(L)\u22651. Thus |L| is nonempty on every smooth geometric fibre, and the unordered residual incidence is fibrewise nonempty. This independently confirms the nonemptiness content of FND-0005 at the level of residual linear systems. Nonemptiness of the reduced-support open on every fibre is not claimed: when h^0(L) jumps or all residual divisors are non-reduced, reduced ordered points may fail on special fibres; denseness of reduced support on free-pencil fibres is deferred to C66-GEO-H0 / C66-GEO-COMP.\n\n## Explicitly not proved (gaps)\n\nG1. Scheme-theoretic equality of the determinantal/Fitting CE rank-drop scheme with the Abel\u2013Jacobi incidence scheme (nilpotents, multiplicities, obstruction complexes). Geometric-point agreement yields only reduced-support coincidence.\n\nG2. Identification W_5\u2245\u03c9_C^{\u22972}(\u2212D) via relative duality O_{P(E^\u2228)}(1)|_C\u2245\u03c9_C\u2297\u03b3^*O(2) (residual form \u0393\u2208|\u03c9_C(\u2212D)|, D+\u0393 canonical). Useful for interpretation but not required for the obstruction identification of Z^{geo}.\n\nG3. h^0(L)-stratification of the relative linear system; components, dimensions, stabilizers, unbalanced CE splittings, hyperelliptic/trigonal special loci; maps to tetragonal moduli (C66-GEO-H0, C66-GEO-COMP).\n\nG4. Any Tate-type, Gysin, or survival statement for W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) or for codimension-13 contributions supported on a closed stratified Z (C66-TATE-*, C66-CEX-*).\n\nG5. Constancy of relative dimension 2 (P^2-bundle) over all of balanced tetragonal moduli: h^0(\u03c9_C(\u2212D))=3 requires h^0(O_C(D))=2 and fails where the pencil jumps.\n\n## Computational hypotheses\n\nNone. No experiment is used; finite experiments cannot close a proof.\n\n## Failed routes addressed\n\n- vcd-only-vanishing: not used; FND-0035 records that ordinary degree 26 equals vcd(M_{6,6}), so vanishing strictly above vcd is inert for the campaign target. The present lemma is purely geometric.\n- undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex: not used; no graph-complex model of lowest weight is invoked.\n- point-count-without-degree-parity-separation: not used; no point-counting enters.\n- forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown: not used; FND-0036 is ambient dictionary only and is not rewritten as a purity theorem for local systems on M_{6,5}.\n\n## Downstream handoff\n\nThe geometric object for later nodes is: residual incidence for L=W_5\u2297\u03c9_C^{-1} over balanced tetragonal CE data, with ordered reduced model a finite \u00e9tale S_6-cover of an open (not closed) reduced-support locus, and with CE failure controlled solely by the f=5 summand.",
